\\ Checked I64 arithmetic: a result outside I64 is a trap, never a wrapped value.
\\
\\ squares emits its argument and calls itself on the square. From 3 the values
\\ are 3, 9, 81, 6561, 43046721 and 1853020188851841; the next square does not
\\ fit in I64, so the multiplication traps and the program ends there. The call
\\ is in tail position, so the recursion does not grow the continuation.
\\
\\ Expected:
\\ > emit "square": 3
\\ > emit "square": 9
\\ > emit "square": 81
\\ > emit "square": 6561
\\ > emit "square": 43046721
\\ > emit "square": 1853020188851841
\\ > outcome: Trap Overflow

(define squares {I64 --> I64}
  n -> (do (emit "square" n)
           (squares (* n n))))

(define main {I64 --> I64}
  input -> (squares (+ input 3)))
